Still hugging her pipa, half her face in shade about.
She tuned the pegs, plucked the strings two, three times—
A feeling born before the melody's first chimes.
Each string, each note, was choked with deep emotion;
She seemed to tell of lifelong thwarted devotion.
With lowered brows, she idly played on, string by string,
Telling all the countless things her heart did bring.
Light pressing, slow twisting, stroking, then a flick—
First the Rainbow Skirt, then the Six Minor quick.
The low strings moaned like sudden rain;
The high strings whispered like secrets in pain.
The two entwined, a tumble, a clash, a blend—
Like great and small pearls falling on a jade dish-end.
Like orioles singing softly under flowers in flight;
Like a spring choked with ice, its flow blocked from light.
The cold ice stiffened, the strings' pulse nearly dead;\nSilence fell, the sound no longer spread.
Yet from that silence, hidden sorrows rose,
That wordless pause spoke more than any sound that flows.
Then—like a silver vase breaking, water bursting out;
Like armored cavalry charging, swords and spears about.
At the song's end, she swept the拨 across the strings with might—
Four strings rang as one, like silk torn clean in the night.
East boats, west barges, all sat hushed, no word;
Only the autumn moon on the river's heart was seen, unstirred.
She tucked the拨 among the strings, sank in thought;\nAdjusted her clothes, composed the face she'd taught.
