For her, I’ve wasted away, worn thin with yearning."}

messages.content:
Spring Gazing  
The nation shattered, yet mountains and rivers remain;  
In spring the city is overgrown with grass and trees.  
Moved by the times, flowers shed tears;  
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.  
War fires burn for three long months—  
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.  
My white hair grows thinner as I scratch my head;  
So thin now, it barely holds a hairpin.

The wind is swift, the sky high, apes wail mournfully;  
Islands clear, sands white, birds wheel and return.  
Endless falling leaves rustle down in autumn's sigh,  
The endless Yangtze roars on, ceaselessly rolling.  

A thousand miles of sorrow in autumn, I wander as a guest;  
A hundred years of illness, I climb this platform alone.  
Hardship and grief have turned my temples to frost;  
In poverty, I’ve just put down my cup of turbid wine.

