Islands clear, sands white, birds wheel and return. 
Endless falling leaves rustle down in a vast sweep, 
The endless Yangtze roars on, surging without end. 
A thousand miles of sorrow in autumn, I wander as a guest; 
A century of illness, alone I climb this tower. 
Hardship and bitter resentment have whitened my temples with frost; 
In poverty and decline, I've just laid aside my muddy wine cup."}

{"translation":"A new verse sung, a cup of wine—
Same weather as last year, the pavilion old.
Westward sinks the sun—when will it return?
Helpless, the flowers fall;
Like old acquaintances, swallows come back.
Alone I pace the fragrant path in the small garden."}

{"translation":"A new verse sung, a cup of wine—
Same weather as last year, the old pavilion.
When will the sun set westward return?
Helplessly, flowers fall;
Like long-remembered swallows come again.
Alone I pace the fragrant path in the small garden."}

{"translation":"A new verse sung, a cup of wine—
Same weather as last year, the old pavilion.
Westward sinks the sun—when will it return?
Helpless, the flowers fall;
Like old acquaintances, swallows come again.
Alone I pace the fragrant path in the small garden."}

{"translation":"I dwell at Yangtze's source, you at its end,
Each day I think of you, yet see you not;
We drink the same river’s water, friend.
How long this flow? How long this grief?
May your heart be like mine—sure and true—
And never break the love we pursue."}

{"translation":"A new verse sung, a cup of wine—
The same weather as last year, the old pavilion.
Westward sinks the sun: when will it return?
Helpless, the flowers fall;
Like old acquaintances, swallows come again.
Alone I pace the fragrant path through the small garden."}

{"translation":"A new verse set to song, a cup of wine,
Same weather as last year, old pavilion stands.
Westward sinks the sun—when will it return?
Helpless, the blossoms fall;
Like memories long known, swallows return again.
Alone I pace the fragrant path through the small garden."}

messages.content:
Spring Gazing  
The nation shattered, yet mountains and rivers remain;  
In spring the city is overgrown with grass and trees.  
Moved by the times, flowers shed tears;  
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.  
War fires burn for three long months—  
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.  
My white hair grows thinner as I scratch my head;  
So thin now, it barely holds a hairpin.

