A hundred years of illness—alone I climb this height.  
Hardship and resentment have whitened my temples with frost,  
Now I’ve just stopped drinking my cloudy wine, in ruin.

The wind is swift, the sky vast—the apes' cries mourn;  
On clear islets, white sands, birds wheel and return.  
Endless falling leaves rustle in the chill;  
The endless Yangtze rolls on without rest.  
A thousand miles of sorrow in autumn, I wander as guest;  
A hundred years of illness—alone I climb this height.  
Hardship and resentment have whitened my temples with frost,  
Now I’ve just stopped drinking my cloudy wine, in ruin.

Spring Gazing  
The nation shattered, yet mountains and rivers remain;  
In spring the city is overgrown with grass and trees.  
Moved by the times, flowers shed tears;  
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.  
War fires burn for three long months—  
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.  
My white hair grows thinner as I scratch my head;  
So thin now, it barely holds a hairpin.

