The wind is swift, the sky high, apes wail mournfully;  
Islets clear, sands white, birds wheel and return.  
Endless falling leaves rustle down in a vast sweep,  
The endless Yangtze roars on in ceaseless flow.  

A thousand miles away, I grieve at autumn's chill, an exile;  
In my hundred years of illness, I climb this tower alone.  
Hardship, bitterness, and hatred have silvered my hair with frost;  
Now impoverished, I've just laid down my cup of turbid wine.

