message.content:
When the nation fell, mountains and rivers remain;  
In spring, the city's grass and trees grow deep.  
Moved by the times, flowers shed tears;  
Hating parting, birds alarm the heart.  
Warfires burn for three long months—  
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.  
White hair scratched, grows even shorter;  
So thin now, it nearly cannot hold a hairpin.

When the nation fell, mountains and rivers remain;  
In spring, the city's deep with grass and trees.  
Moved by the times, flowers shed tears;  
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.  
War flames burn for three long months;  
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.  
My white hair, scratched even thinner,  
Hardly holds a hairpin now.

