message.content:
The wind is swift, the sky vast, apes wail mournfully;  
Islands clear, sands white, birds wheel and return.  
Endless falling leaves rustle down in autumn's sigh,  
The endless Yangtze roars on, ceaselessly rolling.  
A thousand miles of sorrow in autumn, I wander as a guest;  
A hundred years of illness, I climb this tower alone.  
Hardship and bitter resentment, frost-white hair grows thick;  
In poverty, I've just laid down my cup of muddy wine.

When the nation fell, mountains and rivers remain;  
In spring, the city's grass and trees grow deep.  
Moved by times, flowers weep with sorrow;  
Hating parting, birds startle hearts with cries.  

For three months, war flames have burned without cease;  
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.  
White hair, scratched more, grows even shorter—  
So thin now, it nearly cannot hold a hairpin.

