message.content:
A nation shattered, yet hills and streams remain;
Spring fills the city, weeds and trees grow rank.
Moved by the times, flowers draw my tears;
Hating separation, birds startle my heart.
Beacon fires burn through three long months;
A letter from home is worth a thousand gold.
My white hair, scratched, grows thinner still,
Soon too scant to hold a pin.

The nation is shattered, the mountains and rivers remain;
The city in spring, grass and trees run deep.
Moved by the times, flowers splash with tears;
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.
Beacon fires have burned for three spring months;
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.
I scratch my white head, it grows ever sparser;
Soon it will be too thin to hold a pin.

