{"translation":"On the Height

The wind is sharp, the sky is high; the gibbons' cries are mournful.
The islet is clear, the sand is white; birds wheel in flight.

Boundless, the falling leaves rustle and descend;
Endless, the Yangtze's waves roll on and on.

Ten thousand miles from home, in autumn's sadness I've often been a sojourner;
All my life, sick and ailing, I climb this terrace alone.\n
Hardships and bitter regret have thickened the frost on my temples;
Despondent, I've lately stopped my cup of turbid wine."}

{"translation":"On the Heights

The wind swift, the sky high, gibbons wail in sorrow;
The isle clear, sand white, birds wheel in flight.
Boundless, the falling leaves rustle down;\nEndless, the Yangtze rolls on wave after wave.
A thousand miles from home, I often grieve this autumn,
A lifetime sick, I climb the terrace alone.\nHardships and bitter regret have thickened my frosted temples;
Down and out, I have lately stopped my cup of turbid wine."}

{  
  "translation": "Empty hills, no one in sight,
Only the sound of someone talking.
Slanting light enters the deep wood
And shines again on the green moss."  
}

{
"translation": "The wind is fierce, the sky is high, apes cry mournfully;
The islet is clear, the sand is white, birds circle back.
Boundless fallen leaves rustle down;
Endless Yangtze River rolls on.
A thousand miles, I grieve at autumn, forever a guest;\nA hundred years, often ill, I climb the terrace alone.
