{"translation": "The capital lies in ruin; hills and streams remain.
Spring in the city; grass and trees grow wild.
Moved by the times, flowers bring forth tears;
Pained by parting, birds alarm the heart.
Beacon fires have lasted three months;
A single letter from home is worth ten thousand pieces of gold.
I scratch at my thinning hair,
So thin, no hairpin can stay."}

{"translation":"On the Height

The wind is high, the sky is vast, gibbons cry their mournful note;
The islets clear, the sands white, birds wheel about in flight.
Boundless, the falling leaves rustle as they drift and fall;
Endless, the Yangtze’s waves surge on in rolling might.

A thousand miles away, in autumn grief, a stranger ever roaming;\nA hundred years of life, oft sick, alone I climb this height.\nThrough all the hardship and the bitter regret, my temples frost with white;\nDespondent now, I’ve even stopped my cup of turbid wine."}

{"translation":"**Seeing Off Meng Haoran at Yellow Crane Tower on His Way to Guangling**

My old friend bids farewell to the west of Yellow Crane Tower,
In the misty blossoms of March, he sails down to Yangzhou.
The lone sail, a distant shadow, vanishes into the blue void;
All I see is the Yangtze River flowing where sky and water meet."}

messages.content:
The nation shattered, hills and streams remain;
Spring fills the city, grass and trees grow deep.
Moved by the times, flowers bring my tears;
Grieving at parting, birds pierce my heart.
Beacon fires burn for three months on end;
A letter from my family is worth ten thousand in gold.
I scratch my white hair, now too thin and short,
To hold a hairpin any more.

Our land is rent, yet hills and streams remain;
The city springs, where grass and weeds grow rank.
Moved by the times, the flowers splash my tears;
Hating our parting, birds startle my heart.
Beacon fires have burned three months on end;
A letter from my kin would be worth a fortune.
I scratch my white hair, growing ever sparser,
Till it can barely hold a hairpin.

A nation shattered, hills and streams remain;
The city springs, grasses and trees grow wild.
Moved by the times, flowers splash with tears;
Grieved by parting, birds startle the heart.
Beacon fires have burned for three long months;
A letter from home would be worth its weight in gold.
I scratch my white hair, it grows ever sparser,
Soon too thin to hold a pin.

The wind is sharp, the sky is high, apes cry their grief;
The isle is clear, the sand is white, birds wheel in flight.
Boundless, the falling leaves descend with rustling sound;
Endless, the Yangtze’s waves come rolling down in might.
Ten thousand miles away, I grieve for autumn, ever roaming;
A hundred years of illness, I climb this terrace alone.
Hardship and bitter regret have thickened my frosty hair;
Down and out, I’ve just had to stop my cup of cloudy wine.

message.content:
{"translation":"Seeing Meng Haoran Off at Yellow Crane Tower

My old friend bids farewell to the West at Yellow Crane Tower,
Down to Yangzhou in the mist and flowers of March.
A lone sail, a distant shadow, vanishes into the blue;
All I see is the Long River flowing to the sky's edge."}

messages.content:
The wind is high, the sky is vast, the gibbons cry;
On isles the sand is white, as birds wheel by.
Boundless, the leaves come rustling to their fall;
Endless, the river surges past them all.
For miles and miles, a guest in autumn's grief,
A century of ills, alone I climb the peak.
Hardship and hate have frosted thick my hair;
Sorrow has stopped the cup of wine right there.

The wind is sharp, the sky is high, gibbons wail with sorrow;
The isle is clear, the sand is white, birds wheel and circle there.
Endless, the falling leaves come rustling down;
Unending, the great river rolls on its way.
For miles upon miles, in this sad autumn, I am ever a traveler;
Through all my life, beset by sickness, I climb this terrace alone.
Hardship and bitter regret have filled my temples with frost;
Despairing, I set down my cup of turbid wine.

message.content:
{
"translation": "A New Song Sung, A Cup of Wine

A new song sung, a cup of wine,
Last year’s weather, an old pavilion again.
The setting sun descends west—will it ever return?

Helplessly, blossoms fall away;
Familiar, the returning swallows seem.
On the fragrant path of the small garden, I pace alone in lingering."
}

{
"translation": "Spring View

The nation shattered, mountains and rivers remain;
In spring, the city, grass and trees grow deep.
Moved by the times, flowers draw tears;
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.
Beacon fires stretch through three months;
A letter from home would be worth ten thousand in gold.
My white hair, scratched, grows ever sparser;
Soon it will not hold a hairpin."
}

messages.content:
A song of new words, a cup of wine;
The same old pavilion, same weather as last year.
When will the setting sun return?

Helplessly, the flowers fall away;
Familiar, the swallows come again.
I pace alone on the fragrant path of my small garden.

