message.content:
The wind blows sharp, the sky so high, sad gibbons cry;
On isles of white sand, birds circle in the clear sky.
Boundless, the falling leaves descend with rustling sound;
Endless, the Yangtze rolls and surges all around.

Ten thousand miles away, I grieve that autumn’s here;
A lifetime sick, I climb the terrace, all alone.
My troubled life has bleached my hair with frost and fear;
Now, down and out, I’ve even put my wine cup down.

The nation shattered, mountains and rivers remain;
Spring in the city, grass and trees grow thick.
Moved by the times, flowers splash with tears;
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.
Beacon fires stretch through three months;
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.
I scratch my white hair, growing ever sparser,
Soon it will not hold a pin.

