The wind is high, the sky is vast, the gibbons cry in sorrow;
The islet clear, the sand is white, the birds wheel back upon the air.

The boundless falling leaves rustle and descend without end;
The endless Yangtze rolls and surges, ceaseless, coming from afar.

Ten thousand miles away, grieving at autumn, I am always a guest;
A hundred years of illness, I climb this terrace all alone.

Hardship and bitter regret have thickened my temples with frost;
Worn out and down and out, I have just stopped my cup of turbid wine.

