message.content:
At Xunyang River’s head, I bid a guest farewell;
The autumn rustles through the maple leaves and reed.
The host dismounts, the guest boards ship a while;
To raise the cup and drink, we lack the music’s smile.
Too drunk for joy, we face a bitter parting soon;
The river floods with moonlight as we leave the room.
Suddenly, a pipa sounds upon the stream;
The host forgets to go, the guest suspends the dream.
We seek the sound, ask softly who is playing there;
The music stops, she hesitates to answer our prayer.
We move our boats to join, invite her to appear;
Add wine, relight the lamp, and start the feast anew.
After a thousand calls, at last she comes in sight;
Still holding the pipa, half hiding from the light.
She turns the pegs, plucks strings, a few clear notes ring out;
Before a tune is formed, the mood is all about.
Each string a sigh, each note a deep lament;
As if telling of a life of discontent.
With lowered eyes, she plays casually and slow;
Telling of all the things that in her heart do grow.
Light pressing, slow twisting, strumming and picking fast;
First "The Rainbow Skirt," and then "The Six Beats" at last.
The thick strings roar like torrential rain that falls;
The thin strings whisper like private, secret calls.
The roaring rain, the whispering breeze, in chaos mixed;
Like large and small pearls, falling on jade plates fixed.
Like orioles singing sweetly under flowers bright;
Or a hidden spring choking on ice in the night.
The ice-cold spring freezes; the strings snap and cease;
The sound stops, blocked and cold, finding no release.
A hidden sorrow and dark hatred now rise;
At times, this silence speaks better than any sound’s cries.
Like a silver vase breaking, water bursting wide;
Like armored cavalry charging, swords and spears collide.
The song ends with a sweep across the center of the strings;
The four strings crack like silk torn, one sharp sound that rings.
The boats to east and west are silent, no one speaks;
Only the autumn moon, white in the river’s peaks.
She tucks the pick into the strings, deep in thought;
Adjusts her clothes, stands up, a solemn look she’s caught.
She says, "I am a girl from the capital’s bright dome;
My home is near the Toad Hill, my childhood’s home.
I learned the pipa well when I was thirteen years;
My name was listed first among the court musicians’ peers.
When I finished a song, even the masters would admire;
When I dressed up, the autumn beauties’ hearts with envy fire.
The young men of the Five Tombs competed for my favor;
For one song, the red silk rewards I could never number.
The jeweled comb struck the beat till it broke into bits;
My blood-red silk skirt was stained by splashed wine’s fits.
Year after year of laughter, another year of cheer;
I spent the spring winds and autumn moons without a care.
My brother went to war, my aunt passed away;
Day followed night, and my youthful looks began to decay.
Before my door, the horses’ hooves grew few and cold;
Growing old, I married a merchant, as I was told.
The merchant values profit over love, and goes away;
Last month, he left for Fuliang to buy tea today.
He comes and goes, I guard this empty boat alone;
The moon shines bright around the boat, the river’s water, cold.
Deep in the night, I dream of my youth’s fleeting days;
I wake crying, my makeup ruined by tears’ red ways."
Hearing her pipa, I had already sighed;
Hearing her words, I sighed again, deeply cried.
Both of us are outcasts, wandering far from home;
Why must we have met before, for us to understand each other’s gloam?
I left the capital last year, exiled and sick;
Lodging in Xunyang, my health began to prick.
Xunyang is remote; there is no music to be heard;
All year, I do not hear a single string or word.
I live near the Pen River, low and damp the place;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo grow around my space.
What do I hear at dawn and dusk, from day to day?
The cuckoo’s bloody cry, the gibbon’s mournful lay.
On spring days with flowers, or autumn nights with moon;
I often take my wine and drink it alone in tune.
Are there no mountain songs or village flutes to play?
Their harsh, discordant sounds are hard to bear, I say.
Tonight, hearing your pipa, like celestial music bright;
My ears are temporarily cleared, my spirit takes flight.
Please, do not refuse; sit and play one more tune;
For you, I will compose "The Ballad of the Pipa," a gift to you soon.
Moved by my words, she stands for a long time, then sits;
She tunes the strings tighter, and the tempo fits.
The sound is mournful, not like the music heard before;
The whole audience, hearing it again, weeps, unable to ignore.
Who among us shed the most tears in this place?
The River Prefect, his blue official gown, wet from the embrace.

