候选1：The nation is shattered, yet mountains and rivers remain;
Spring fills the city, where grass and trees grow wild.
Moved by the times, flowers draw our tears;
Grieved by parting, birds startle our hearts.
Beacon fires have burned for three months unbroken;
A letter from home would be worth its weight in gold.
I scratch my white hair, it grows thinner still,
Soon it will be too thin to hold a pin.
候选2：A broken state, but hills and streams remain;
Spring in the city, grass and trees run deep.
Moved by the times, flowers bring forth tears;
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.
Beacon fires have burned for three long months;
A letter from home is worth ten thousand gold.
I scratch my white hair, it grows ever thinner,
Soon too scanty to hold a hairpin.
候选3：Spring Gazing

The nation shattered, yet mountains and rivers remain;  
In spring the city is overgrown with weeds and trees.  
Moved by the times, flowers shed tears;  
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.  
War's flames burn for three long months—  
A letter from home is worth ten thousand pieces of gold.  
My white hair, plucked again and again, grows ever thinner—  
So thin, it barely holds a hairpin.
候选4：Spring Gazing

The state lies shattered, yet mountains and rivers remain;  
In spring, the city is overgrown with grass and trees.  
Moved by the times, flowers shed tears;  
Hating parting, birds startle the heart.  

War beacons blaze for three long months—  
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.  
White hair plucked more and more, grows thin—  
Almost too sparse to hold a hairpin.

