候选1：The wind is sharp, the sky is high, the gibbons' cries are mournful;
The islet clear, the sand white, birds circle in their flight.
Boundless, the withered leaves come rustling down;
Endless, the Great River rolls its way.
Ten thousand miles away, in sorrowful autumn, I am ever a sojourner;
A hundred years of sickness, I climb this terrace alone.
Hardship and bitter regret have thickened the frost upon my temples;
In my destitution, I have but now set down my cup of turbid wine.
候选2：The wind is sharp, the sky is high, the gibbons cry their woe;
The isle is clear, the sand is white, the birds wheel to and fro.
The boundless woods shed leaves that rustle down with every breath;
The endless Yangtze rolls its tides to meet the world beneath.
A guest who grieves for autumn days ten thousand miles away,
I climb the terrace all alone, sick all my life and gray.
My hardships and my bitter grief have frosted o'er my hair;
Down and out, I’ve just now stopped the cup of turbid liquor here.
候选3：The wind is swift, the sky vast, apes wail mournfully,  
Islands clear, sands white—birds wheel and return.  
Endless falling leaves rustle down,  
The endless Yangtze rolls in mighty surge.  
A thousand miles of sorrow in autumn, I wander afar,  
In a century of illness, alone I climb this high terrace.  
Hardship and hatred have whitened my temples with frost,  
Now newly stopped drinking muddy wine.
候选4：The wind is swift, the sky high, apes wail mournfully;  
On clear islets, white sands, birds wheel and return.  
Endless fallen leaves rustle down in waves;  
The endless Yangtze rolls on, surging forward.  
A thousand miles of sorrow in autumn I've long been a guest;  
In life's brief span, ill and alone I climb this tower.  
Hardships and bitter regrets have whitened my temples with frost;  
In poverty and decline, I've just laid down my muddy wine cup.

