message.content:
{"translation":"A Song of the Pipa Player

On the Xunyang River bank at night I saw a friend off;
Maple leaves and reed flowers rustled in the autumn chill.
I dismounted while my guest stepped into the boat;
We raised our cups to drink, but there was no music.
Drunk yet not merry, we parted in sorrow;
At parting, the vast river was flooded with moonlight.
Suddenly from the water came the sound of a pipa;
I forgot to go home, and my guest would not depart.
Following the sound, I asked in a low voice who was playing;
The music stopped, and she hesitated to answer.
I moved my boat nearer and invited her to appear;
We added wine, relit the lamps, and reopened the feast.
After a thousand calls she finally came out,
Half hiding her face behind the pipa she held.
She turned the pegs and plucked the strings a few times;
Before a melody formed, feeling already stirred.
Every string was muted, every note full of thought,
As if telling of a life of unfulfilled hopes.
Brow lowered, she played on with ease,
Telling all the endless matters in her heart.
Lightly strumming, slowly pinching, sweeping and picking,
First the Rainbow Skirts, then the Six Yao.
The great strings roared like sudden rain;
The small strings whispered like private talk.
Roaring and whispering, tangled together,
Like great and small pearls falling on a jade plate.
Like orioles singing smoothly beneath the flowers,
Like a hidden stream choking beneath the ice.
The ice-cold stream congealed, the strings snapped silent;
The silence held, and the sound paused for a while.
A secret sorrow and hidden resentment rose;
At that moment, silence spoke louder than sound.
Then a silver vase shattered, water bursting forth;\nIron cavalry charged, swords and spears clashing.
The song ended with a sweep across the heart of the instrument;
All four strings snapped as one, like tearing silk.
Boats east and west were hushed, no one spoke;
Only the autumn moon shone white on the river's heart.
She pondered, tucked the pick into the strings,
Straightened her clothes, rose, and composed her face.
"I was once a girl of the capital,
My home was near Hama Ling.
At thirteen I mastered the pipa,
My name ranked first in the Music Bureau.
After each song, even skilled players admired me;
When dressed up, I was envied by the beauties.
Young men of the Five Tombs fought to reward me;
For one song, I received countless red silks.
Ornamental combs broke as I beat time;
My blood-red silk skirt was stained with wine.
Year after year of laughter and song,
The spring moons and autumn winds passed idly by.
My younger brother went to the army, my aunt died;
Days and nights passed, and my beauty faded.
Before my door, carriages grew sparse and cold;
Growing old, I married a merchant.
A merchant values profit over parting;
Last month he went to Fuliang to buy tea.
I wait alone at the river mouth in an empty boat;
The bright moon encircles the boat, the river water cold.
In the deep night I suddenly dream of my youth;
I wake weeping, my rouge-streaked tears staining my cheeks."
I had sighed at the pipa's first song;
Hearing her words, I sighed again and again.
Both of us are wanderers at the world's end;
Why must we have met before to understand each other?
Since last year I left the capital's glory;
Sick and bedridden, I dwell in Xunyang.
The land is remote, with no fine music;
All year I never hear the sound of strings and flutes.
I live near the River Pen, low and damp;
Yellow reeds and bitter bamboo grow around my house.
What do I hear here, morning and evening?
The cuckoo's bloody cries, the gibbons' mournful wails.
On flower-filled spring mornings and moonlit autumn nights,
I often drink alone, pouring cup after cup.
Are there no hill songs and village flutes?
Their crude, grating sounds are hard to bear.
Tonight I hear your pipa's song;
It is like fairy music, and my ears are briefly revived.
Do not refuse—sit and play one more song;
For you, I will compose this Song of the Pipa Player.
Moved by my words, she stood still for a long while;
Then sat back, tightened the strings, and they grew urgent.
The mournful notes were unlike those before;\nEveryone in the seats wept, covering their faces.
Who wept the most among the guests there?
The River Prefect's blue robe was soaked with tears."}

