Wind swift, sky high, gibbons cry with sorrow;
Islet clear, sand white, birds circle back.

Boundless, the falling leaves shudder down;
Endless, the great river rolls on.

Ten thousand miles, a guest in autumn's grief;
A hundred years, I climb this terrace, sick.

Hardship and bitter regret whiten my temples;
Dispirited, I newly stop my cup of wine.

The wind is high, the sky is vast, gibbons cry in sorrow;
The islets clear, the sand is white, birds wheel and fly about.
Boundless, the falling leaves come rustling down;
Endless, the Yangtze surges rolling on its way.
Ten thousand miles away, grieving at autumn, I’ve long been a guest;
In a hundred years, with many an illness, I climb this terrace alone.
Hardship and bitter hate have thickened the frost upon my temples;
In my wretchedness, I have lately stopped my cup of turbid wine.

The wind is swift, the sky is high, the gibbons cry in grief;
The islet clear, the sand white, the birds circle back beneath.

The boundless leaves come rustling, rustling down;
The endless river rolls, rolls on without cease.

Ten thousand miles, a guest in this sad autumn;
A hundred years, with ills, I climb the terrace alone.

Hardship and bitter regret have thickened frost in my temples;
Dispirited, I have newly stopped my cup of turbid wine.

