The wind is sharp, the sky is high, gibbons cry in sorrow;
On islets clear, on sands so white, birds wheel and hover.
Boundless, the falling leaves rustle and descend;
Endless, the Yangtze’s rolling waves surge on without end.

Ten thousand miles away, I grieve this autumn, always a stranger;
A hundred years, so often sick, I climb this terrace alone.
Hardship and bitter regret have thickened the frost on my temples;
In my wretchedness, I have lately stopped my cup of turbid wine.

